2026-09-12T09:07Z claude note found while implementing 0771: PR #530 claims 0.3.44 invisibly to the guard, exactly as an open PR's ticket ID is invisible to erg check
2026-09-12T09:47Z claude note Rule 3 implemented on claude/audit-zotero-plugin-u8iot3-0779, PR #533: the guard now reads every open pull request head through one replaceable command (bench/forge_open_prs.py, marked harness-extension-point, overridable with SITTER_FORGE_COMMAND) whose three-form protocol answers about at most one PR per invocation, so no caller can express the one-shot list-plus-filter. Fails when another open PR claims the working tree version over a DIFFERENT payload; a head already an ancestor of HEAD is left to rule 1, which is what stops a merged PR being double-reported and every lane reddening against its own PR. Unreachable forge, refusal or no forge remote read NOT-RUN on their own line beside a green exit, with five fixtures: rules 1 and 2 stay fully evaluated and make check stays runnable offline, per this ticket's own invariant. Positive control against the live forge named PRs #531 and #530 at 0.3.45 and suggested 0.3.46; it caught two first-draft defects, an all-clear line printed above its own FAILs and a suggestion of 2.11.2 that would have undone the 2026-09-06 scheme reset. make check is NOT green here: one pre-existing failure, test_permission_denied_directory_is_reported_unreadable, which reproduces on a pristine origin/main tree because this container runs as uid 0. No .github/workflows exists, so nothing runs any of this on a push and the docstring says so. Ticket file added to this branch at the coordinators direction after 32d26f7b removed it from #531, since erg-pr-merge closes the ticket named on its own PR. Closed header untouched: the merge decision is a verification seats.
--- body ---

## Context

Found while implementing 0771, and it is the same blind spot the repository
already documents for ticket IDs.

`bench/check_sitter_version.py` rule 1 — no two payloads answer to one manifest
version — is anchored at the tip of the *current* branch and reads
`git log <path>` in this checkout. A version claimed by an **open pull request**
on another branch is invisible to it. Today: PR #530 bumps the sitter to 0.3.44
on `claude/deja-mis-ou-pas-xuyotp`; a parallel lane that also took 0.3.44 would
pass the guard on its own branch, and the collision would only exist once both
merged — by which time the two payloads that shared a number are both in
history, which is the one state the guard's own docstring calls unfixable.

0771 avoided it by hand: it took 0.3.45 after reading #530's diff. That is not a
guard.

`tickets/AGENTS.md` describes exactly this shape for ticket IDs, names the
enumeration trap (`gh pr list --json files` does not populate `files`, so a
one-shot list-plus-filter returns empty regardless of content), and isolates the
forge-specific part behind `# harness-extension-point`. The harness's
`scripts/check-cross-pr-ticket-collision.sh` is the model.

**This repository has no `.github/workflows/`**, so nothing runs on a push here:
the guard is only as good as a lane running `make check`. Say that in the
docstring rather than implying CI coverage that does not exist.

## Relevant files

- `bench/check_sitter_version.py` — rules 1 and 2, and the NOT-RUN discipline
- `tests/test_check_sitter_version.py` — the fixtures, including every NOT-RUN
- `tickets/AGENTS.md` — the enumeration trap and the extension point convention

## Actions

1. Add a cross-branch reading: for each open pull request, the manifest version
   its head carries, and fail when another open PR's head already claims the
   working tree's version. Keep it behind a `# harness-extension-point` call so
   the forge-specific enumeration is one function.
2. Enumerate, then query per PR. Never a one-shot list-plus-filter whose empty
   answer cannot be told from "I could not look".
3. **NOT-RUN, never green, when the forge cannot be reached** — no network, no
   credentials, not a clone of a forge-hosted repo. This guard's own docstring
   already holds that line for four cases; this is a fifth.
4. Before trusting it, run it against a case known to be positive: today's tree
   plus #530's 0.3.44 is that case.

## Test

- Red first: a fixture where another open PR claims the working tree's version
  and the guard passes.
- The positive case fails with the PR named and a next free version suggested.
- An unreachable forge reads NOT-RUN, with its own fixture, like the other four.
- A version claimed by a *merged* PR is rule 1's business already and must not
  be double-reported.

## Verification

- [x] `python3 -m pytest tests/test_check_sitter_version.py -q` — 29 passed.
- [ ] `make check` — NOT discharged, and not by anything in this change: it
      ends `1 failed, 1334 passed, 32 skipped` on
      `test_permission_denied_directory_is_reported_unreadable`, which
      reproduces identically on a pristine `origin/main` tree in the same
      container (the session runs as uid 0, so its `chmod 000` denies
      nothing). Every other guard is green, `sitter-version` included. Left
      unticked rather than argued green; a reviewer on a non-root runner is
      what can tick it.
- [x] The new check shown failing against the real case before it is trusted.
      The version is 0.3.45, not the 0.3.44 this ticket was written with:
      #530's head had moved by the time the control ran, and #531 had taken
      0.3.45 as well, so the guard named BOTH open pull requests and suggested
      0.3.46. Neither number is current as of the commit that writes this
      line — #530 stands at 0.3.45, #531 has moved to 0.3.46 — and the
      control is recorded as what it observed when it ran, not as a live
      claim.

## Invariants

- The four existing NOT-RUN cases keep answering NOT-RUN.
- Rule 2's release-agreement half is untouched.
- No network call is required for the local rules: a lane offline still gets
  rules 1 and 2, and a NOT-RUN line for the third.

## Exit criteria

- Two lanes cannot ship two payloads under one version without one of them
  being told, before the merge that makes it permanent.
